    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Aging is accompanied by functional decline and an increase in many diseases, including neurodegenerative and cardiovascular diseases. As the demographics shift towards older populations world-wide, a better understanding of the aging process is crucial to develop therapeutics that could extend the healthy portion of life. A host of mechanisms contribute to the complex process of aging, including epigenetic modifications, nutrient signaling pathways, and protein homeostasis pathways. Epigenetic modifications that affect chromatin state, including histone modifications, are of particular interest as age-related changes to chromatin state often occur later in life. Thus, targeting the conserved enzymes that add or remove histone modifications has the potential to impact a wide range of processes. One of these enzymes, UTX-1, is a demethylase that acts on trimethylated lysine 27 on Histone H3 (H3K27me3). Consistent with an important link between chromatin and longevity, deficiency in UTX-1 extends the lifespan of the worm Caenorhabditis elegans. Another enzymatic complex involved in longevity is COMPASS, the methyltransferase complex that trimethylates lysine 4 on Histone H3 (H3K4me3). When members of this H3K4me3 complex, such as the methyltransferase ASH-2, are knocked down, the lifespan of C. elegans is increased. However, the mechanism of action, precise tissues of relevance, effects on healthspan, and relevant initiation times are unknown for these lifespan-extending modifications. Here I show that knockdown of utx-1 and ash-2 via RNAi can still extend lifespan in C. elegans when initiated in old adults compared to young adults. Furthermore, knockdown of utx-1 or ash-2, either at young adulthood or late-adulthood, increases thermotolerance (resistance to heat stress) and improves mobility throughout life. I next sought to gain a greater understanding of the molecular mechanism of UTX-1 mediated longevity regulation. I find that contrary to its role in development, the lifespan-extending phenotype of utx-1 deficiency is dependent on UTX-1 demethylase activity. In addition, I have found that UTX-1 acts in several tissues to mediate lifespan extension, but that full lifespan extension can only be obtained when UTX-1 is deficient in more than one tissue. Together, these results indicate the feasibility of impacting healthspan and lifespan in already old individuals through knockdown of evolutionarily conserved chromatin modifiers.",
